.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Setting a trap with an empty command string resets the signal to its
default disposition (equivalent to \f[CR]trap \(aq\(aq SIGNAL\f[R] in
bash, which ignores the signal; use \f[CR]bc_state_unset_trap()\f[R] to
fully reset to the default handler).
.IP \(bu 2
The DEBUG and RETURN pseudo\-signal traps are inherited by shell
functions only if the function has the \f[CR]trace\f[R] attribute or the
\f[CR]functrace\f[R] shell option is set.
.IP \(bu 2
The ERR trap is not inherited by shell functions by default.
Set the \f[CR]errtrace\f[R] shell option (\f[CR]set \-E\f[R]) for
inheritance.
.IP \(bu 2
Trap commands execute in the context of the session\(cqs shell
environment.
They have access to all variables, functions, and state of the session.
.IP \(bu 2
The function uses the v2 binary protocol internally.
The STATE channel request includes \f[CR]\(dqop\(dq: \(dqset\(dq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(dqns\(dq: \(dqtrap\(dq\f[R], \f[CR]\(dqname\(dq\f[R] (signal
name), and \f[CR]\(dqvalue\(dq\f[R] (command) fields.
.IP \(bu 2
A single \f[CR]bc_client_t\f[R] must not be used concurrently from
multiple threads.
